WEBVTT
NOTE Error Analysis and Measurement

1
00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:03.500
In this video, we will be talking about
types

2
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.600
of uncertainties, significant figures, and
how

3
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.300
to calculate with uncertainties. Why do
uncertainties

4
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.700
matter, first of all? Well, uncertainties
matter

5
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.700
because no physical measurement in real
life

6
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.940
is exact. Every single measurement is
limited

7
00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:26.360
by something. For example, instrument
precision,

8
00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:41.940
or experimental technique, or

9
00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.020
environmental errors.

10
00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:54.600
So, environment. And because physics
relies

11
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.920
on a lot of measured data, each value has
to

12
00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.220
include an uncertainty. And without it,
you

13
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:05.380
can't really do it effectively.

14
00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:11.960
A measurement is written as x equals x
measured

15
00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:15.280
plus minus the uncertainty.

16
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.700
For example, let's say you want to measure
the

17
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.560
length of a book. You could say the length
would

18
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:31.160
be equal to 12.4 plus minus 0.1
centimetres.

19
00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:34.400
And this is the uncertainty.

20
00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.320
This basically tells the readers, or the
examiner,

21
00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.900
that your book has a length between 12.3
and

22
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:51.160
12.5. There are two types

23
00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:54.720
of uncertainties. There's random
uncertainty

24
00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:01.120
and systematic uncertainty.

25
00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.300
Both of these are really stressed in IB.

26
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:13.200
If you're doing AP, this is probably not
going

27
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.460
to be that important. But if you're doing
IB

28
00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:18.760
and you're going to be doing labs, this is
very,

29
00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:22.420
very important to know. Random uncertainty
is

30
00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:26.240
caused by unpredictable fluctuations.

31
00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.200
And it varies from trial to trial.

32
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:40.210
For example, when you're doing a
stopwatch,

33
00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:43.670
there could be a difference in reaction
time.

34
00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:50.370
However, systematic uncertainty is a
consistent

35
00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:55.050
error, or a consistent bias, in one
direction.

36
00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:01.550
And it can't be reduced by repetition,
while

37
00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:03.750
random can be reduced by repetition.

38
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:10.730
Can't be reduced by repetition.

39
00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:17.990
An example of this could be a ruler that
has

40
00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:22.750
a missed painting, or the etch marks on
the

41
00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:31.470
ruler are not even. Etch marks on ruler
are

42
00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:35.490
not even. So basically, systematic
uncertainty

43
00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.870
skews all your data in one direction,
while

44
00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:41.910
a random uncertainty is basically the more
you

45
00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:44.570
do the experiment, the less random
uncertainty

46
00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:49.320
there will be. How do you estimate
uncertainty

47
00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.260
from instruments? How do you actually get
the

48
00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:59.040
instruments? Well, for analog instruments,
which

49
00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:02.160
means things that you can read, for
example,

50
00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:06.720
a ruler. Or, a good thing in chemistry
would

51
00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.800
be a graduated cylinder. You could read
the

52
00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.380
etch markings. It would be plus-minus-half
the

53
00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:22.620
smallest division. So what do I mean by
this?

54
00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:28.620
Well, let's say this ruler goes up by one
centimetre,

55
00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:31.420
as most rulers do.

56
00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:33.940
So when you're measuring it, the
uncertainty

57
00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.840
of whatever you're measuring with this
ruler

58
00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:40.780
would be plus-minus-0.5 centimetres.

59
00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:45.040
And this would be your uncertainty.
However,

60
00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:49.180
let's say you're measuring with a digital
instrument.

61
00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:52.580
Digital instrument.

62
00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.520
And the uncertainty would be plus-minus
the

63
00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.880
least significant figure.

64
00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:03.760
Least sig dig.

65
00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:09.900
So basically, in this case, it means that
you

66
00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:15.560
want to plus-minus one of the furthest
decimal

67
00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:18.520
places. So let's say you have a stopwatch,
and

68
00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:22.680
your stopwatch measures, let's say, 2.36.

69
00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:26.820
Well, the least decimal place here would
be

70
00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:29.820
two after the one.

71
00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:35.740
So the uncertainty here would be 0.01
seconds.

72
00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:40.620
And that's basically how to get the
uncertainty

73
00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:43.040
in an experiment. You're going to probably
learn

74
00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:45.540
this in class for IB, but it's good to
know

75
00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:51.750
earlier. Now, how do you calculate with
uncertainties?

76
00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:57.010
Let's say you want to calculate the
velocity

77
00:05:57.010 --> 00:06:03.130
or speed of an object. Velocity is
distance

78
00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:11.740
over time. And let's say your distance is

79
00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:18.760
measured as 5.0 plus-minus one metres.

80
00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.640
And your time using your stopwatch could
be,

81
00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:28.640
let's use this, 2.36 seconds plus-minus
0.01

82
00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:34.680
seconds. So when you're multiplying or
dividing

83
00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.740
uncertainties, and this is really, really
important

84
00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:42.120
in labs for both physics and chemistry,
you

85
00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:45.280
want to first take the percentage
uncertainty.

86
00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:50.280
If you're not doing IB or high school and
you're

87
00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:52.260
focusing on AP, you don't really need to
worry

88
00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:57.260
about this. This is for lab work. So let's
first

89
00:06:57.260 --> 00:07:00.320
focus on the distance. The percent
uncertainty

90
00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:05.920
for distance would be one, let's say, or
0.1

91
00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:08.100
metres would be a more reasonable
uncertainty.

92
00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:13.600
So 0.1.

93
00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.880
So the percent uncertainty here would be
0.1

94
00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:20.700
over 5.0 times 100%.

95
00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:28.650
And when you do that, you would get a
percent

96
00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:30.950
uncertainty of 2.0%.

97
00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:39.760
Same thing for the time, and you get 0.01
over

98
00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:43.760
2.36 times 100%.

99
00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:47.840
And that would be equal to 0.01 divided by
2.36

100
00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:51.820
times 100. The total uncertainty would be
0.4,

101
00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:55.120
0.42.

102
00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:00.360
Sorry, 0.4, yeah, 0.4%.

103
00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:06.240
And how do you get the total uncertainty?

104
00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:09.160
Well, this is what you have to do.

105
00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:12.800
You just add them. Once you have all the
uncertainties

106
00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:15.300
required for division or multiplication,
you

107
00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:18.960
could just add the uncertainties. And the
total

108
00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:20.980
uncertainty would be 2.4%.

109
00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:24.320
So once you calculate what your final
velocity

110
00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:31.160
is, you would get 2.1 metres per second.

111
00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:35.420
You would take this 2.4 uncertainty and
you

112
00:08:35.420 --> 00:08:42.060
would put it into here. So if you do this,
divided

113
00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:46.460
by 100 times 2.1, you get an uncertainty
of

114
00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:50.920
plus minus 0.04 metres per second.

115
00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:55.540
And that would be your final answer.

116
00:08:56.060 --> 00:09:00.240
Make sure that your final digit also
matches

117
00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:03.600
the uncertainty here. So you would have to
add

118
00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:07.640
an extra figure here. But basically that's
how

119
00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:10.160
you calculate uncertainties when you're
dividing

120
00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:12.740
and multiplying. What if you're adding two
values?

121
00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:16.060
Let's say you want to add two distances,
5.0

122
00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:22.080
plus minus 0.1 metres, 0.1 metres.

123
00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:24.900
And then you add that with another
distance

124
00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:29.040
calculation, let's say 6.0 plus minus 0.2
metres.

125
00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:33.260
Well, when you're adding or subtracting,
it's

126
00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:35.160
way easier than when you have to multiply
and

127
00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:36.860
you have to get all the percent
uncertainties

128
00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:39.640
and adding them. You could just add the
uncertainties

129
00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:42.860
directly. Because if you think about it,
these

130
00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:47.460
uncertainties cover the ranges of what you
have

131
00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:50.560
to measure. So by adding them, you are
accounting

132
00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:55.040
for both the ranges. And that's how you
calculate

133
00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:58.660
uncertainty. Remember, for multiplying and
dividing,

134
00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:01.260
you have to convert it to percent
uncertainties,

135
00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:04.280
add it, and then apply it to your final
answer.

136
00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:06.520
However, for adding, you could just
directly

137
00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:09.940
add the uncertainties. I hope this will
give

138
00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:13.180
you a head start on lab work, and I hope
this

139
00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:14.640
helped you. Thank you.
